        "question": "Using Lagrange Multipliers, find the maximum and minimum values of $f(x,y) = xy$ subject to the constraint $x^2 + y^2 = 8$.",
        "answerKey": "Using Lagrange Multipliers (grad f = lambda * grad g):\n1. f(x,y) = xy => grad f = (y, x)\n2. Constraint g(x,y) = x^2 + y^2 - 8 = 0 => grad g = (2x, 2y)\n3. Equations:\n   y = lambda * 2x  (Eq. 1)\n   x = lambda * 2y  (Eq. 2)\n   x^2 + y^2 = 8    (Eq. 3)\n4. From Eq 1 & 2, if x or y = 0, then both = 0, which violates Eq 3. So x, y != 0.\n   lambda = y / 2x = x / 2y => y^2 = x^2 => y = +/- x.\n5. Substitute into Eq. 3:\n   x^2 + x^2 = 8 => 2x^2 = 8 => x^2 = 4 => x = +/- 2.\n6. Critical points: (2, 2), (2, -2), (-2, 2), (-2, -2).\n7. Evaluate f(x,y) at these points:\n   f(2, 2) = 4 (Maximum)\n   f(-2, -2) = 4 (Maximum)\n   f(2, -2) = -4 (Minimum)\n   f(-2, 2) = -4 (Minimum)\n8. Max value is 4, Min value is -4."
